\description{
Draws \code{x} in a self-contained HTML page, in the CRS chosen by
\code{\link[=view_crs]{view_crs()}} unless \code{crs} is given, and returns the view. Printing the
view in an interactive session opens the page in the IDE's viewer or the
browser. The page is written by \code{\link[aobcore:write_scene_html]{aobcore::write_scene_html()}} and needs
no server and no network: the data travel in the page as native
'GeoArrow'. A view with large local rasters is served from a local
server instead (see Transport).
}
\details{
Points, lines and polygons (single or multi) are drawn as one layer
each. A mixed \code{GEOMETRY} or \code{GEOMETRYCOLLECTION} column is split into up
to three layers, polygons at the bottom, then lines, then points. Empty
geometries are dropped, as are Z and M values. The split layers share
the view's name, with the kind appended to each label, as in
\code{"mixed (polygons)"}.

\strong{Inputs.} Vector data are read through 'wk' (allboa/design decision
0008): \code{x} can be any geometry vector 'wk' can handle (an \code{sfc},
\code{\link[wk:wkb]{wk::wkb()}}, \code{\link[wk:wkt]{wk::wkt()}}, \code{\link[wk:xy]{wk::xy()}}, \code{\link[wk:rct]{wk::rct()}}, a 'geos' geometry, a
'geoarrow' vector, ...), or a data frame with such a column, whose other
columns are the attributes (for \code{zcol} and popups). An \code{sf} data frame is
one; for any other data frame the geometry column is the first that 'wk'
can handle, or the one named by \code{geometry}. The CRS travels with the
geometry (\code{\link[wk:wk_crs]{wk::wk_crs()}}). A terra \code{SpatVector} is read from terra's own
WKB (see \link{view-terra}). A \code{\link[wk:grd]{wk::grd()}} is not drawn yet: a grid belongs on
the raster path.

'aobcore' does not reproject, so \code{x} is transformed to the view CRS here
by 'PROJ' (\code{\link[wk:wk_transform]{wk::wk_transform()}} with \code{\link[PROJ:proj_trans_create]{PROJ::proj_trans_create()}}). When
\code{x} is in lon/lat and the view CRS differs, lines and polygon edges are
first densified in lon/lat (every \code{densify} degrees, 0.25 by default,
with \code{\link[aobcore:vector_densify]{aobcore::vector_densify()}}), so an edge along a parallel curves as
it should in a polar view instead of cutting across it. The transform is
point by point: nothing is cut at the antimeridian or the poles, so the
view is right in any CRS but the topology of features that cross a
seam is not guaranteed. A point PROJ cannot transform (one beyond an
orthographic view's horizon, say) leaves its feature out, with a
warning.

\strong{Colour by attribute.} \code{zcol} names a column of \code{x} whose values
colour each feature: the fill of polygons and points, the stroke of
lines. Numbers take a continuous palette over their range (or one colour
per class with \code{breaks}), factors, character and logical values one
colour per level; \code{NA} takes \code{na_colour}. The colours are computed in R
by \code{\link[=view_colours]{view_colours()}} and travel to the page as one RGBA column beside the
geometry (with the popup columns, below). With \code{zcol}, polygons get a grey
outline (change it with \code{stroke}); \code{fill}, and \code{stroke} for lines, are
errors, since \code{zcol} sets those colours.

\strong{Legend.} With \code{zcol}, the view carries a legend for the colours, built
from the same \code{\link[=view_colours]{view_colours()}} result as the features, so the two agree:
a ramp over the range for numbers, one entry per interval with \code{breaks}
(labelled as in \code{"(5, 10]"}), or one entry per level. An \code{"NA"} entry is
added only when some drawn feature took \code{na_colour}. A column with more
than 30 levels gets no legend (with a message), since a key that long is
not readable; the features are still coloured. \code{legend = FALSE} leaves it
out. A legend is scene spec 0.5 data (see \code{\link[aobcore:scene_add_legend]{aobcore::scene_add_legend()}}).

\strong{Popups.} \code{popup = TRUE} (the default) carries \code{x}'s attribute columns
in the page and declares them as the layer's popup (scene spec 0.5):
selecting a feature (a click, tap or key press) shows its values. Only
the first 20 columns are carried, with a message, since every column adds
bytes to the page; choose columns with \code{popup = c("a", "b")}, which has
no cap, or carry none with \code{popup = FALSE}. A data frame with no
attribute columns gets no popup. Numbers, character and logical columns
are carried as they are; factors as their labels, \code{Date} as
\code{"YYYY-MM-DD"}, \code{POSIXct} and \code{POSIXlt} as ISO 8601 text in UTC, and
other atomic classes as their \code{as.character()} text. List, raw and matrix columns
cannot be shown and are left out with a message.

\strong{Minimal style.} \code{style = "minimal"} draws with the least the page
carries and the renderer builds per feature, for exploring how much data
a view can take (like \code{pch = "."} in base graphics): opaque one-pixel
lines and polygon outlines, polygons not filled (so not triangulated),
points as two-pixel dots with no outline, and no popup columns unless
\code{popup} asks for them. Without \code{zcol}, each colour is one constant for
the layer, not a value per feature. \code{fill}, \code{stroke}, \code{stroke_width_px}
and \code{radius_px} still apply on top (a \code{fill} fills polygons again). With
\code{zcol}, the column colours polygon outlines rather than fills. An
unfilled polygon is selected (in a popup or from a served page) only by
its outline, not by a click inside it; give \code{fill} for that. The geometry itself
is unchanged: every vertex still travels in the page as 16 bytes (two
doubles), plus a third for base64.

\strong{Transport.} A view is embedded (the page above, on disk) or served
from a local HTTP server by \code{\link[aobcore:serve_scene]{aobcore::serve_scene()}}, which the browser
reads a local COG from tile by tile instead of carrying its bytes in the
page (allboa/design decision 0006). \code{transport = "embed"} always embeds,
\code{"serve"} always serves (it needs the 'httpuv' package), and \code{"auto"}
(the default, or \code{getOption("aobview.transport")}) embeds unless the
view's local raster tiles, counted from each layer's tile plan before
any byte is read, come to more than \code{getOption("aobview.embed_max")}
bytes (32 MiB by default). Then, in an interactive session with 'httpuv'
installed, that layer and any added later are served, with a message
naming the size; otherwise the tiles are embedded with a warning. Vector
data and remote COGs never make a view served by themselves. A served
view keeps its server running until \code{v$server$stop()},
\code{\link[aobcore:stop_scene_servers]{aobcore::stop_scene_servers()}} or the end of the R session; the server
answers only while R is idle. A temporary COG (see \link{view-terra}) of a
served layer is kept in \code{file.path(tempdir(), "aobview-cogs")} until
the server stops.

\strong{Selections.} A served view's page sends the features the viewer
selects (a click, Shift-click to add or remove) back to R: every vector
layer can be selected. Read them with \code{\link[=selection]{selection()}}, \code{\link[=selected]{selected()}} and
\code{\link[=wait_for_selection]{wait_for_selection()}}; an embedded page cannot send them.

\strong{Spec version.} The scene is written at the lowest scene spec version
that can express it (\code{\link[aobcore:scene_spec_version]{aobcore::scene_spec_version()}}): a view with no
legend and no popup is unchanged by these features.
}
\examples{
# Any geometry 'wk' can read, with its CRS: no 'sf' needed.
line <- wk::wkt("LINESTRING (0 -60, 90 -60)", crs = "OGC:CRS84")
v0 <- view(line)
v0$scene$view$crs

# A data frame with a geometry column.
bases <- data.frame(base = c("Casey", "Davis"))
bases$geom <- wk::xy(c(110.53, 77.97), c(-66.28, -68.58), crs = "OGC:CRS84")
v1 <- view(bases, zcol = "base")
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("sf", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
coast <- sf::st_read(system.file("extdata", "coastline_south_40s.geojson",
                                 package = "aobcore"), quiet = TRUE)
v <- view(coast)
v$scene$view$crs
file.exists(v$file)

nc <- sf::st_read(system.file("shape", "nc.shp", package = "sf"), quiet = TRUE)
v2 <- view(nc, crs = "EPSG:26717", fill = c(200, 120, 40, 160))
v3 <- view(nc, zcol = "BIR74", palette = "YlOrRd")
v4 <- view(nc, zcol = "SID74", breaks = c(0, 5, 10, 20, 50))
v4$scene$legends[[1]]$classes[[1]]$label
v5 <- view(nc, zcol = "BIR74", popup = c("NAME", "BIR74"))
v5$scene$layers[[1]]$popup
v6 <- view(nc, style = "minimal")
v6$scene$layers[[1]][c("stroke", "stroke_width_px", "fill")]

# CCAMLR statistical areas (simplified, illustrative; see the extdata
# README) in EPSG:6932, coloured by area with popups, viewed in EPSG:3031
areas <- sf::st_read(system.file("extdata", "ccamlr_statistical_areas.geojson",
                                 package = "aobview"), quiet = TRUE)
areas$area <- paste0("Area ", substr(areas$GAR_Long_Label, 1, 2))
v7 <- view(areas, crs = "EPSG:3031", zcol = "area",
           popup = c("GAR_Name", "GAR_Long_Label", "GAR_Start_Date", "GAR_Size"))
v7 <- view_add(v7, coast, popup = FALSE)
vapply(v7$scene$legends[[1]]$classes, function(cl) cl$label, "")
\dontrun{
v2
v3
}
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
